I think that nethack should allow you to stop whatever you are doing
whenever *anything interesting* happens (but it doesn't).  Ideally,
interruptable activities would include picking locks, eating, running,
and commands prefixed with a numerical count.  If only I had a zorkmid
for every time I typed "500." and fainted in the middle of it because
nethack didn't think that warning messages like "you are getting weak"
were important enough to distract me from the important nothing that I
was doing.  Ideally one would be able to interrupt long activities
with ^G or ^C.  Then, instead of typing space at the --more-- prompt
you could type ^G or ^C.  Of course, if some important message fit on
a single screen you could still be screwed.

As a second order consideration, some people might want to ignore
certain messages like "you hear a guard on patrol" so a list of
messages to ignore or even suppress would eventually be nice.

Of course, it is important to define "anything interesting".  I think
that anything that causes a message to appear is interesting, up to
first order.  How many times have got the message "You read ad
a??ae?ium", looked down and found yourself in a doorway?  How many
times have you been patiently waiting for something exciting to happen
(having typed 100.) and had a rust monster sneak up (while you were
obviously distracted by the all-consuming act of doing nothing) and
trash your armor?  Wouldn't it be nice if your multiple-turn command
were interrupted when the despicable Mr. R entered the room?  I guess
I have been spoiled by playing urogue too much.  Never mind.

